---
const mapping = [
  { from: "git branch", to: "A draft" },
  { from: "preview deploy", to: "A preview link to share" },
  { from: "pull request", to: "Send for review" },
  { from: "merge to main", to: "Publish" },
  { from: "git commit", to: "A checkpoint you can undo" },
  { from: "coding agent", to: "Ask in plain English" },
  { from: "frontmatter", to: "A form with real fields" },
];
---

<section class="bg-sand" id="features">
  <div class="page grid gap-12 py-section md:grid-cols-12 md:gap-10">
    <div class="md:col-span-4">
      <h2 class="text-3xl tracking-display text-ink md:text-4xl">The repo stays. The jargon doesn't.</h2>
      <p class="mt-5 max-w-measure text-muted">
        Content teams are told to learn branches, reviews and deploys. None of that is the work. Orso keeps the same
        files, history and deploy, and gives the people writing the words a surface they can actually use.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-8 border-t border-line pt-5 text-sm text-muted">
        One repository. One source of truth. No migration, no second system to keep in sync.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="md:col-span-7 md:col-start-6">
      <div class="grid grid-cols-2 gap-4 border-b border-line pb-3 text-sm font-medium text-muted">
        <p>In your repo</p>
        <p>In Orso</p>
      </div>
      <ul>
        {mapping.map((row) => (
          <li class="grid grid-cols-2 items-baseline gap-4 border-b border-line py-4">
            <span class="font-mono text-xs text-muted sm:text-sm">{row.from}</span>
            <span class="text-ink">{row.to}</span>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ul>
      <p class="mt-5 text-sm text-muted">Same files. Same history. Same deploy.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
